###
This tests an issue that can come up in Java bytecode.
After the first branch of the 'if', there is a branch target with a stack map frame that contains the local 'l'.
At the top of the 'until', there is also a branch target where 'l' should not be present.
Since the 'if' has an empty 'else' expression, these would normally be the exact same position.
To make this work, a noop is inserted so that they can have different stack maps.
###
test
	foo is true
-foo bool()
	done mut = false
	_ void =
		l = done
		if l then ()
	until done
		done := true
	done
